The **CEA Card** is run by the Cinema Exhibitors' Association. It is accepted at most cinemas in the UK.

## What it gives you

When you buy a **full price ticket** for yourself, you get **one free ticket** for someone to go with you and help you. The tickets must be for the same film, showing and screen, bought at the same time.

## Who can get one

You can usually apply if you get:

- **PIP** (either part)
- **DLA** (any rate)
- **Attendance Allowance**
- **Armed Forces Independence Payment**

You can also apply if you are registered **blind or sight impaired**. If you do not get these benefits, the CEA may accept other evidence, such as a letter from your GP or consultant.

## How much it costs and how to apply

The card costs **£6** at the time of writing. Apply online at the **CEA Card website** and upload your proof, such as your benefit award letter.

{% callout type="good" title="Tip" %}
Many cinemas also run **autism-friendly** and **subtitled** showings. Check your local cinema's accessibility page.
{% /callout %}
